# Regret and alternate histories
#
# `regret` does not rewind.  It forks.
#
# When Alice regrets a choice, the runtime keeps the road not taken as a live
# branch beside the timeline she is actually in.  The branch is not simulated --
# it is *valued*.  Its worth is pinned to how well Alice was doing at the moment
# of the fork, and then it drifts upward every tick, because an unlived option
# has nothing to disappoint you with.  That drift is the grass-is-greener term,
# and it is the entire mechanism.
#
# Her live regret is the gap:
#
#     regret = valuation(alternate) - wellbeing(actual)
#
# So regret grows when the life she is in goes badly, shrinks when it goes well,
# and never goes away on its own -- it has to be closed with `acceptance`, which
# costs grief.  Both histories stay computationally accessible the whole time.

alice = agent
bob = agent

love alice bob 0.7
have alice home 1

choice alice "leave" "stay"
regret alice

tick 8

# The actual history gets worse. Notice what that does to the road not taken:
# nothing at all, which is exactly why regret climbs.
lonely alice 0.6
sadness alice 0.5

tick 8
remember alice

# A second choice, and a second fork. Regret is not one slot.
choice alice "come back" "keep going"
regret alice
tick 6

remember alice

# Acceptance closes the branches. It does not make them false -- it makes them
# finished, and charges grief for the difference.
acceptance alice
tick 4

remember alice
observe alice
